<?php
/**
 * Spartrak Logo.
 *
 * Overrides Porto's own Magento_Theme::html/header/logo.phtml (theme fallback:
 * spartrak -> porto) for one reason: the SpareTrak brand asset.
 *
 * WHY NOT getLogoSrc(): Magento\Theme\ViewModel\Block\Html\Header\
 * LogoPathResolver reads the `design/header/logo_src` store config and, when
 * that is empty, falls back to the theme asset `images/logo.svg`. Neither
 * exists for this project (no Admin logo was uploaded, and this brand's asset
 * is a raster PNG, not an SVG), so getLogoSrc() resolved to a missing file and
 * the storefront rendered Porto's own stock "porto eCommerce" placeholder
 * logo instead — CONFIRMED on the live Arabic store, 2026-08-24.
 *
 * Per the project brand rule (skills/elassal/SKILL.md): SpareTrak is the
 * consumer-facing storefront brand — every string/asset a shopper sees — while
 * ElAssal is the company/legal entity. So the storefront header carries the
 * SpareTrak mark, NOT skills/elassal/assets/elassal-logo.png (the corporate
 * ElAssal mark, correct for invoices and corporate identity only).
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * TWO LOCKUPS — BOTH EXPORTED FROM FIGMA, NEITHER AUTHORED HERE
 * ===========================================================================
 * images/spartrak-logo.png  135x152, 32.9KB — the mark-dominant stacked
 *   lockup. Derived from the 1024x1024 transparent PNG source fill behind
 *   Figma node 735:32550, auto-trimmed of its padding and resampled to 2x the
 *   Figma header render size (68.24 x 75.42) so it stays crisp on retina
 *   without shipping a 400KB image. Proportions VERIFIED against the 1440
 *   export: Figma renders 67x75 with a 60px mark over a 67-wide wordmark; this
 *   asset is 135x152 with a ~122px mark over a full-width wordmark — the same
 *   80/15/5 mark/wordmark/gap split.
 *
 * images/spartrak-logo-compact.png  164x110, 19.8KB — Figma's OWN 2x export
 *   of node 724:25837, the mobile lockup. It is NOT the desktop asset scaled:
 *   Figma builds the mobile version from two independently-scaled clipped
 *   crops of the same source, producing a small mark over a wide wordmark
 *   (mark 38x41, wordmark 80x13, 82x55 overall) where the desktop lockup is
 *   mark-dominant. Scaling the desktop asset to fit either axis would have
 *   missed the other by ~35px, so the mobile lockup is committed as its own
 *   Figma export rather than re-proportioned by hand.
 *
 * Served through <picture media>, so each breakpoint downloads exactly ONE
 * raster and never both — mobile pays 19.8KB instead of the desktop 32.9KB, so
 * this is a net saving on the smaller connection, not an added cost.
 *
 * BOTH <source> and <img> carry explicit width/height. That is the layout-shift
 * guard: without width/height on the <source>, the browser would reserve the
 * <img> attributes' 68:75 box for the mobile image and then reflow to 82:55
 * once it decoded — a visible jump in the header on every mobile page load.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * SCOPE OF THE COMPACT VARIANT
 * ===========================================================================
 * This one template also serves the FOOTER logo instance (node 693:45786,
 * 113x125), which is mark-dominant like the desktop header and has no
 * Figma-specified mobile variant. The <picture> wrapper is therefore opt-in
 * via the `compact_logo_src` block argument, set only on the header instance
 * in Smartwave_Porto/layout/default.xml. Without it this template emits the
 * same single <img> it always did, so the footer is untouched by this pass.
 *
 * Dimensions come from the block's own logo_img_width/logo_img_height
 * arguments, set per-instance in that same layout file (68x75 header per node
 * 693:45441, 113x125 footer per node 693:45786), so one template serves both
 * placements without hardcoding either size.
 *
 * Deliberately always renders a link, unlike Porto's version which swaps to a
 * non-clickable <strong> on the homepage — Figma shows no such distinction and
 * a clickable logo is the stronger convention.
 *
 * @var \Magento\Theme\Block\Html\Header\Logo $block
 */
$storeName = $block->getThemeName() ?: $block->getLogoAlt();

/**
 * QA FIX (2026-08-27) — PER-INSTANCE LAYOUT ARGUMENTS WIN OVER STORE CONFIG.
 *
 * These two lines used to prefer $block->getLogoSizeResolver() and fall back
 * to the block's own arguments. That is backwards for this theme, and it was
 * measurably wrong on the live site: the resolver reads the GLOBAL
 * design/header/logo_width|logo_height store config, which still holds Porto's
 * stock 111 x 44, so BOTH logo instances rendered width="111" height="44" —
 * neither the header's 68x75 (node 693:45441) nor the footer's 113x125 (node
 * 693:45786) that Smartwave_Porto/layout/default.xml passes per instance.
 *
 * Those attributes are the CLS guard: the browser reserved a 111x44 box and
 * then reflowed to whatever the stylesheet resolved once the raster decoded —
 * a visible jump on every page load, on the two elements that sit at the very
 * top and very bottom of every page.
 *
 * A per-instance layout argument is a deliberate, specific statement about ONE
 * placement; the store config is a single global value that cannot describe two
 * differently-proportioned lockups at once. So the argument is now authoritative
 * and the resolver is the fallback for any instance that sets no argument —
 * which preserves the admin-configurable behaviour everywhere it is still the
 * only source.
 */
$logoSizeResolver = $block->getLogoSizeResolver();
$logoWidth = $block->getLogoWidth()
    ?: ($logoSizeResolver !== null ? $logoSizeResolver->getWidth() : null);
$logoHeight = $block->getLogoHeight()
    ?: ($logoSizeResolver !== null ? $logoSizeResolver->getHeight() : null);

$logoSrc = $block->getViewFileUrl('images/spartrak-logo.png');
$logoAlt = $block->getLogoAlt() ?: 'SpareTrak';

// Opt-in compact (mobile) lockup — header instance only. The intrinsic CSS
// size of images/spartrak-logo-compact.png, which is Figma's 2x export of the
// 82x55 mobile lockup.
$compactLogo = $block->getData('compact_logo_src');
$compactWidth = 82;
$compactHeight = 55;
?>
<a
    class="logo"
    href="<?= $block->escapeUrl($block->getUrl('')) ?>"
    title="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr($storeName) ?>"
    aria-label="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr(__('SpareTrak home')) ?>"
>
    <?php if ($compactLogo) : ?>
    <picture>
        <source
            media="(max-width: 767px)"
            srcset="<?= $block->escapeUrl($block->getViewFileUrl($compactLogo)) ?>"
            width="<?= /* @noEscape */ $compactWidth ?>"
            height="<?= /* @noEscape */ $compactHeight ?>"
        />
        <img
            src="<?= $block->escapeUrl($logoSrc) ?>"
            alt="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr($logoAlt) ?>"
            <?= $logoWidth ? 'width="' . $block->escapeHtmlAttr($logoWidth) . '"' : '' ?>
            <?= $logoHeight ? 'height="' . $block->escapeHtmlAttr($logoHeight) . '"' : '' ?>
        />
    </picture>
    <?php else : ?>
    <img
        src="<?= $block->escapeUrl($logoSrc) ?>"
        alt="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr($logoAlt) ?>"
        <?= $logoWidth ? 'width="' . $block->escapeHtmlAttr($logoWidth) . '"' : '' ?>
        <?= $logoHeight ? 'height="' . $block->escapeHtmlAttr($logoHeight) . '"' : '' ?>
    />
    <?php endif; ?>
</a>
